WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:09.800
Hey, Wisecrack.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:10.340
Jared, here.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.860
It all started when someone at the office
said, “Jared.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.500
You gotta watch The Boss Baby.”

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:22.820
So, I did.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.970
And then, perhaps reluctantly, we covered
it on our movie podcast, Show Me The Meaning!

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.570
And, yes, shameless plug —&nbsp;check it out
using the link in the description.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.410
And let me tell you - something odd happens
to you when you spend hours thinking about

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:36.410
The Boss Baby.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.090
Soon, it became a semi-ironic obsession.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.590
And then, after it got nominated for an Oscar,
we said, “F**k it!

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:44.470
We have to do a video on The Boss Baby.”

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.870
So, we’re doing something a little different
today.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:52.040
It’s not that The Boss Baby is a deliberate
and nuanced exploration of childhood psychology.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:56.540
It’s more: holy shit, this movie accidentally
shows us how the way we currently think about

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.820
love is not only ruining us, but our future
children.

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:03.899
So, if you thought the Tide Pod challenge
was bad for kids, strap in for this Wisecrack

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.750
Edition on The Boss Baby.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.540
And as always, and if you care: spoilers ahead.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:09.890
Let’s start with a recap.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.500
Tim is living a perfect childhood with his
family.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.799
It’s nothing but imaginary adventures, and
expensively licensed lullabies — "Blackbird

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:19.770
singing in the dead of night.”

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.670
Until… the baby shows up.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.850
Suddenly, Tim’s parents need to spread the
love around, and Tim finds himself struggling

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.000
with not being the center of attention.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.080
“What about me?!”

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.000
Tim learns that this is no ordinary baby — it’s
an Oscar Nominated Boss Baby.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.939
It can talk, hold meetings, and eat solid
food.

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:41.420
At first, Tim tries to expose the baby’s
bossy ways, thinking a besuited baby might

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.270
freak his parents out enough that they…

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.759
I dunno throw the baby away or something.

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:50.220
But the boss baby isn’t going out like that
and outwits Tim at every turn.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:55.420
Tim learns the baby is only around to foil
Puppy Co.’s mysterious new puppy that threatens

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.060
to make babies obsolete, somehow.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.890
So, he agrees to help.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:03.780
Tim and the Oscar Nominated Boss Baby sneak
into Puppy Co. to conduct some corporate espionage.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.270
Their madcap adventure goes off the rails,
and they end up in Las Vegas saving their

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.780
parents from a rocket ship with the help of
a tidal wave of puppies.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.780
It’s weird.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.440
So, what’s the movie really about?

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.380
Well, the short answer is easy.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.840
It’s about love.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.790
Tim wants his parents’ love, The Boss Baby
wants to win the market share of love back

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.330
from those gosh dang puppies, and the villain
wants to steal love away from babies.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:38.650
"No one will ever want a baby ever, ever again!"

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:40.440
It all comes back to love.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.739
In the end, Tim and The Boss Baby have a delightful
reunion when they realize that love is not

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:48.280
a finite resource; there’s enough for everyone.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.989
Now, that’s a nice message and all, but
anyone who’s ever had a high school crush

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:55.520
knows we don’t always get the love we want.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:56.959
Now, I know — who cares?

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:00.629
A silly kids’ movie has a reductive view
on love, so what?

00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:03.739
Well, this view on love isn’t just simplistic.

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:05.270
Some might call it dangerous.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:09.050
Like known weirdo, and everyone’s second
favorite psychoanalyst, Jacques Lacan.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:12.880
One of Lacan’s fundamental ideas is the
idea of Lack.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.690
In short, Lacan says that we spend our lives
looking for meaning, unity, and peace, but

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:19.370
those aren’t real things.

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.879
We’re never going to find them, and the
desperate search for them is what makes people

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:24.879
neurotic.

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:29.470
We develop social and psychological disorders
to cope with the fact that despite how badly

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.489
we want life to be perfect, it never will
be.

00:03:32.489 --> 00:03:36.190
And it’s this lack that defines Tim’s
struggle in the film.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:40.030
For Lacan, it all starts with a childhood
trauma called “the mirror stage.”

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:44.130
When we’re babies we don’t really know
what the hell is going on around us, but everything

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.130
is awesome.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:50.050
We’re fed, we’re taken care of, and it’s
all good, but eventually we see ourselves

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:55.440
in a mirror and realize we’re not part of
a comfy, cozy world, but just one little being

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.319
in a giant world.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:03:58.519
That realization hurts.

00:03:58.519 --> 00:04:01.330
It’s when we realize we’re on our own.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:05.799
We see this phenomenon play out in Boss Baby
too, albeit a little differently.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:10.771
Tim is naive to the fact that the world doesn’t
cater to his every need until the Boss Baby

00:04:10.771 --> 00:04:12.849
shows up, and he is traumatized.

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:17.989
Suddenly, the world isn’t all about Tim,
his parents only have a finite amount of love

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:18.989
to give —

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:20.619
“Mom and Dad don’t even know you!

00:04:20.619 --> 00:04:21.619
They love me!”

00:04:21.619 --> 00:04:22.619
“Oh, yeah?

00:04:22.619 --> 00:04:23.619
Do the math, kid.

00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:26.120
There is only so much love to go around.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:32.139
It’s… like these beads: you used to have
all your parents love, all their time, all

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:37.650
their attention, you had all the beads…
but then I came along.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.650
Babies take up a lot of time.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.449
They need a lot of attention.

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:42.949
They get all the love.”

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:44.449
“We could share.”

00:04:44.449 --> 00:04:46.860
“You obviously didn’t go to business school.”

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.600
This theory that there isn’t enough love
for everyone is the foundation of every Oscar-nominated

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.389
choice the Boss Baby makes.

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:55.090
Tim tries to live with the baby.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:59.499
But he can’t — it f**kin’ sucks — so,
he tries to expose him, so his parents will

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:00.499
get rid of him.

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:01.499
No dice.

00:05:01.499 --> 00:05:05.569
He even jumps over a train and sneaks onto
an Elvis-themed flight to Vegas, all to make

00:05:05.569 --> 00:05:07.430
this baby go away.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:08.430
Why?

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.389
Because the baby interrupted his notion of
how love works.

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:12.389
“Life was good.

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:13.590
Life was perfect.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:20.879
But as I drifted off to sleep, something my
parents said got me thinking: where do babies

00:05:20.879 --> 00:05:21.879
come from?”

00:05:21.879 --> 00:05:26.090
No matter how much this feeling of having
your ideal reality shattered may suck, it’s

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.110
an essential stage of human development.

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:32.889
However, instead of having Tim move past this
difficult stage, the film doubles down on

00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:37.660
his childish fantasy - suggesting that love,
is, in fact, infinite.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:41.389
Tim writes a letter to the Boss Baby saying
that there’s plenty of love for both of

00:05:41.389 --> 00:05:43.719
them, as long as they have each other.

00:05:43.719 --> 00:05:45.150
It’s heartwarming.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:50.029
But for Lacan, desire for love, or anything
else, is insatiable because the world will

00:05:50.029 --> 00:05:51.159
never be perfect.

00:05:51.159 --> 00:05:53.840
It is the inevitable outcome of lack.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.569
We’ll never have the flawless, complete
life we want, so we focus on specific things

00:05:58.569 --> 00:06:03.279
as objects of desires, or on individual problems
as the source of our suffering.

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:09.220
In other words, Tim’s idea that love can,
or should be, infinite, is a fantasy.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:11.550
And boy, does Tim love fantasy.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.580
Throughout the movie we see him creating fantasies
to represent the world around him.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:19.919
He uses those fantasies to entertain himself,
to show his feelings, and to grapple with

00:06:19.919 --> 00:06:20.990
with how love works.

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:26.089
In the end, we learn the whole movie is just
a story adult Tim is relaying to his daughter

00:06:26.089 --> 00:06:28.009
to prepare her for a sibling on the way.

00:06:28.009 --> 00:06:29.729
“Is that a true story, Daddy?”

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:32.789
“Well, sweetie, that’s how I remember
it!

00:06:32.789 --> 00:06:34.169
But you know what I found out?”

00:06:34.169 --> 00:06:35.169
“What?”

00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:36.940
“There’s plenty of love for everyone.”

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.099
Lacan might say this poses a few issues.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:44.580
By creating this bizarre narrative about a
ruthless corporate baby who threatens to close

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.119
on all the market share of love in his family
unit, Tim is essentially demonizing his little

00:06:49.119 --> 00:06:53.339
brother to deal with the fact that love is
not, in fact, infinite.

00:06:53.339 --> 00:06:57.289
But whether finite or not, we’re always
going to want more, and we’ll never get

00:06:57.289 --> 00:06:58.800
all the love we want.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.599
This targeting of a single cause for all our
problems is something that Lacanian scholars,

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:06.860
and also regular people, refer to as “scapegoating.”

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:11.089
In the case of the family, we develop neuroses
to cope with the feelings of isolation that

00:07:11.089 --> 00:07:12.900
start with the mirror stage.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.830
Or we fabricate a story about a giant corporation
of babies to justify hating our little brother.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:22.009
We see the same thing happen with the villain
Francis Francis, formerly super colossal big

00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:23.009
fat boss baby.

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:27.710
He becomes an evil psychopath and tries to
steal love from all babies, because his fantasy

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:32.659
of staying a baby forever is taken away from
him, and he blames all other babies.

00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:36.580
This is what happens when we expect a perfect
world and don’t get what we want.

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.209
We act like babies.

00:07:38.209 --> 00:07:40.539
But not necessarily the oscar-nominated kind.

00:07:40.539 --> 00:07:45.929
And then Tim does quite possibly the most
irresponsible thing ever: he passes his fantasy,

00:07:45.929 --> 00:07:49.279
and all the neurotic baggage that comes with
it, on to his daughter.

00:07:49.279 --> 00:07:53.189
Inundating your daughter with the idea that
your basic desires will always be fulfilled

00:07:53.189 --> 00:07:55.489
is… well, not great.

00:07:55.489 --> 00:07:59.370
That will probably lead to some issues down
the line… like whatever happened to this

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:02.980
kid: “Dude, you’re blue collar as f**k,
like, get the f**k out of my face.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:03.880
You make no money.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:05.240
Look, what are you wearing?

00:08:05.249 --> 00:08:06.249
You’re disgusting.”

00:08:06.249 --> 00:08:07.629
So, what’s the deal?

00:08:07.629 --> 00:08:11.230
Is it just that love is another one of the
many things we’re programmed to want, but

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:12.900
doomed to never get enough of?

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:14.879
“Not so fast,” Lacan might say.

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:19.009
There’s in fact a secret way around the
problem of love and fantasy, and it’s also

00:08:19.009 --> 00:08:22.060
hidden in the Oscar Nominated story of The
Boss Baby.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:26.069
What the movie’s ending gets right is that
the love between the brothers is the healthiest

00:08:26.069 --> 00:08:29.939
relationship we see in the movie, but that’s
not because love is infinite.

00:08:29.939 --> 00:08:33.649
Instead, it’s because love depends on showing
us how much we’re missing.

00:08:33.649 --> 00:08:36.419
“You don’t even know what it’s like
to be a part of a family!”

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:38.330
“And you don’t know what it’s like to
have a job!”

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:42.349
Lacan famously referred to love as “to give
what one does not have.”

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:45.529
When we love someone, we reflect their Lack
back to them.

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:50.350
We show them what they are missing, and for
Lacan that confrontation with Lack is far

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:55.759
more psychologically curative than covering
over our desires with fantasies and scapegoats.

00:08:55.759 --> 00:08:59.540
If we accept that definition of love, it’s
hard to argue that the brothers don’t live

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.540
up to it.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.880
From the very beginning they’re positioned
not just as opposites, but as complementary.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.810
Boss Baby is practical, but Tim is a dreamer.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:10.620
The Boss Baby writes memos, Tim takes action.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.470
Tim has experienced love and values it immensely.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.740
Boss Baby has never been loved, and isn’t
too worried about it.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.259
“I may look like a baby, but I was born
all grown up.”

00:09:20.259 --> 00:09:21.971
“You never had someone to love you?”

00:09:21.971 --> 00:09:24.459
“Well, you can’t miss what you never had.”

00:09:24.459 --> 00:09:28.810
They each cover the other’s flaws and reflect
them back to each other in a way that helps

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.889
them both grow and save the day.

00:09:30.889 --> 00:09:35.259
It’s the Boss Baby’s motivational skills
that keep Tim on his bike once he loses the

00:09:35.259 --> 00:09:36.259
training wheels.

00:09:36.259 --> 00:09:40.480
“The path to success is not a straight line,
Templeton, but rather a wild ride!”

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.000
And it’s Tim’s imagination that allows
them to defeat Francis Francis, in the end.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.899
By showing the other what they’re missing,
and embodying those values, they help each

00:09:48.899 --> 00:09:52.190
other grow into more well-rounded and healthy
individuals.

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:56.470
For Lacan love isn’t meant to fill the gaps
we feel in our lives.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:01.291
The attempt to make life perfect is exactly
what leads to neurosis and pathology and all

00:10:01.291 --> 00:10:03.170
kinds of other messy things.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:07.420
Instead, love shows us what we’re missing
and makes it okay, because we see what is

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:09.160
missing in someone else.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:13.130
Maybe it seems like we’re going a little
overboard on a movie about a baby who is,

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:17.509
somehow, paradoxically, a boss, but this is
an important lesson.

00:10:17.509 --> 00:10:23.000
Real life is full of disappointment, and few,
if any, things live up to the world we imagine,

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:25.380
but that’s no reason to get depressed.

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:29.079
All you need to do is realize that things
aren’t gonna be perfect.

00:10:29.079 --> 00:10:33.360
Not everyone gets nominated for an Oscar,
and once you see that imperfection reflected

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.360
in the world around you… it’s really not
so bad.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:37.360
Thanks, Wisecrack.

